&lt;p&gt;It looks pretty complex, but I assure you, it&apos;s not really so bad once you&apos;ve got your head around it. If you&apos;ve never seen an HTML document, then yes, this looks pretty daunting, but it&apos;s a simple tag system. &amp;lt;p&amp;gt; signifies the start of a paragraph, for instance. Each &quot;opening tag&quot; has a closing tag that looks like this: &amp;lt;/p&amp;gt;. Tags must always match.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Anyway, from there you can simply use the toolset to do the following (the first line is the command, the rest are the tool&apos;s output):&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;figure class=&quot;highlight&quot;&gt;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-bash&quot; data-lang=&quot;bash&quot;&gt;tools/gengalleys.sh ./Eve.xml
